WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.570
- Good afternoon.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.160
Welcome to everyone.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.780
My name is Victoria Stone-Cadena,

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.650
I am the associate director here at RITM.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.480
As part of our mission as a center,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.580
we are tasked with
supporting research efforts

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.900
across the university

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.760
that center around issues of race,

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.370
indigeneity, and transnational migration.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.950
In that effort, RITM supports research

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.020
and conference attendance
for both undergraduate

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.150
and graduate students throughout the year.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.890
We are delighted to welcome you all

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.750
for this three minute research showcase,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.300
as we hear from Yale graduate students

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:37.380
who conducted research

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.450
with our support this past summer.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.520
Each student is asked to synthesize

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.230
their research in under three minutes,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.510
using just one slide.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.090
While challenging, it is always exciting

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.650
to hear the highlights

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.960
of important research being
done across the campus,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.450
which includes researchers tonight from...

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.290
Or this afternoon,

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.390
from over 11 different departments,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.140
as you can see in the program here.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.560
After each student presents,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.480
we will have time for
one to two questions,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.700
and then we'll invite the next speaker.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.230
We invite you to join us afterwards

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.780
for some light refreshments
and conversation.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.490
I would like to take a moment

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.041
to give a special thanks to Ryan Huynh,

00:01:14.041 --> 00:01:17.340
who is a Woodbridge Fellow
with RITM this year

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.010
for all his work and
preparation of this event.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.270
Likewise, I'd like to thank

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.410
the rest of the RITM team

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.030
for their support as well.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.260
In keeping this timely,

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.957
I will now turn this over to Henriëtte.

00:01:30.956 --> 00:01:31.789
Thank you.

00:01:31.789 --> 00:01:34.956
(audience applauding)

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.032
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:01:41.032 --> 00:01:43.470
Thank you, Ryan and RITM for organizing,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.910
and for giving me the opportunity

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.590
to offer you a little glimpse

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.140
of what I've been working on this summer.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:49.980
On this slide,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.710
you see an engraving by Abraham Bosse

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:56.022
of actors performing at the
Hôtel de Bourgogne in Paris.

00:01:56.022 --> 00:01:57.630
It's the most prominent permanent stage

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.252
in the 1630s.

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:03.270
Notice how the actor's bodies
are opened up to the audience.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.760
The way the performers are
arranged shows consideration

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.110
for stylized physicality

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.700
and the stage picture.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.020
Notice, too, the costumes,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.190
the different styles.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.080
The captain with the huge ruff,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.720
the lovers and their lavish outfits.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.900
Materials that likely had
origins way beyond France,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.810
testament to France's
growing colonial empire.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.430
Finally, notice the unembellished

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.870
and small stage.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.130
All this relates to a tradition

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.810
of traveling companies,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.490
when costumes and props were easier

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.020
to move than sets,

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.510
and where physicality and
visuality were important

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.550
when voices didn't carry.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.610
Significantly, this image
depicts not just characters

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:39.930
but real people.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.160
The actors you see here were some

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.920
of the most celebrated
French actors of their time,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.930
and many of them performed
not only in Paris,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.190
but also across the country,

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.950
and as far as Belgium and the Netherlands.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.360
For example, Julien Bedeau

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.430
and his wife, Isabel Mestivier,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.310
led the troop of the Prince of Orange

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.739
some 10 years earlier

00:02:57.739 --> 00:02:59.940
before this image was drawn,

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:00.960
bringing their performances

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:02.070
to the court in the Hague

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.825
and to other cities
across the Low Countries.

00:03:04.825 --> 00:03:07.050
The performance cultures
of the Netherlands

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.630
and France were closely connected.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:10.463
In the archives,

00:03:10.463 --> 00:03:13.140
I found significant
similarities in language

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.730
and content of court orders

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.040
that gave actors permission to perform,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.354
as well as in the size
of the playing spaces.

00:03:19.354 --> 00:03:22.050
Furthermore, these were
actors working in Paris,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.070
as well as across France

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:23.910
and the Low Countries,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.280
bringing with them style and technique.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.020
Thus, by studying French sources,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.610
I was able to discover more about

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.950
what performance in the
Hague might've looked like,

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:32.970
and thus what the performance

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.100
of Pierre Corneille's
"Cid" could have been like

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.070
when it was performed there in 1638.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.810
While chiefly presented as a play

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.527
about impossible love,

00:03:41.527 --> 00:03:43.350
"The Cid" is a political adaptation

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.420
of a medieval Spanish legend;

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.060
A response to the centralization
of power in France

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.160
by Richelieu and Louis XIII,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.210
the war with Spain,

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:54.000
and France's expanding colonial project.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.770
My dissertation explores how four

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.420
of the most popular productions

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:01.050
of the first decade of Amsterdam
City theater transformed

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.180
as they transferred from across Europe,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.650
and what implications that has

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.870
on an understanding of
Dutch imperial narratives

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.763
and identity formation at the time.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.250
This semester, I am considering

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.941
the purpose and consequences

00:04:12.941 --> 00:04:14.310
of "The Cid's" circulation.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.630
How would audiences in The Hague,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:16.830
and subsequently Amsterdam,

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:19.547
relate to this story of
a Spanish national hero

00:04:19.547 --> 00:04:21.060
at a time when both France

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:21.893
and the Netherlands were

00:04:21.893 --> 00:04:23.040
at war with Spain?

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:23.910
How could the different

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:26.040
local contexts have informed reception?

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.740
And finally, how might
the character reflect

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.570
a supranational, white, Christian,

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.520
European self-fashioning in opposition

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.660
to the Islamic empires

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.580
to the east and the south?

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.080
If you have any thoughts or questions,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.675
I would love to hear from you.

00:04:38.675 --> 00:04:39.741
Thank you.

00:04:39.741 --> 00:04:42.908
(audience applauding)

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:47.693
- Thank you.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:49.373
Hi everybody.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.323
All right.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.280
So I study ancient religious texts,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.690
especially the Hebrew Bible,

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.965
within the Religious Studies Department.

00:04:59.965 --> 00:05:03.570
And what about that I'm most interested is

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:05.790
contemporary interpretive approaches

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:10.500
that communities develop
towards meaning and praxis.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.870
So even more fascinating about that is

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.570
the power dynamics that come into play

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.730
in the interpretation process,

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.050
noting the fact that

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.450
the predominant interpretive approaches

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.250
and frameworks for a long time have

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.590
all been white Eurocentric.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.120
So some of the key questions for me,

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:28.200
beyond what the Bible...

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.400
What is the Bible,

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.840
is how do we read it?

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.340
And thus, what other non-Western
approaches are there?

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.770
So taking a post-colonial approach,

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.190
my research explores
indigenous knowledge systems,

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:45.870
namely African oral culture,
literature, history,

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.700
including proverbs, riddle...

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.020
Riddles, sorry.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.360
Story forms, novels, cultural songs,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.499
myths, mythologies, et cetera

00:05:54.499 --> 00:05:58.980
to explore new frameworks
that there might be

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:00.749
to capture African realities,

00:06:00.749 --> 00:06:04.440
while also contributing
to the ongoing discussion

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.510
on decentering whiteness

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.090
and multi-racializing
biblical interpretation

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:10.260
and biblical studies.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:12.270
So with support from RITM,

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:13.530
I head to Ghana,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:15.450
a West African country

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.490
with a rich cultural heritage

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.340
and an important history colonization...

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:23.760
Colonialism, independence, and religion.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:24.930
And my research took me

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:27.270
to various institutions and sites

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:29.310
to observe and to talk to key scholars

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.410
already doing similar work.

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.520
So to name a few,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.260
some of the pictures you see here,

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:35.610
I went to the University of Ghana,

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.237
I went to the Bible Society
of Ghana, Ashesi University,

00:06:38.237 --> 00:06:41.537
Akrofi-Christaller Institute
of Theology and Mission,

00:06:41.537 --> 00:06:43.590
Methodist University,
Trinity Theological Seminary.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:46.050
And aside from spending hours talking

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.270
to several scholars and graduate students

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:49.350
at these institutions,

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.080
I audited classes, visited libraries,

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.279
spent some time at various archives.

00:06:54.279 --> 00:06:56.640
And outside of the university world,

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.144
I went to the Kwame Nkrumah Museum,

00:07:00.144 --> 00:07:02.010
in the middle picture,

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.900
to explore Ghana's west...

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:05.762
Ghana and West Africa's history.

00:07:05.762 --> 00:07:09.360
I also went to the slavery...

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.330
The Cape Coast Slave Castle

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:13.590
to explore Africa's history

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.530
with slavery, and
colonization, and independence,

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.080
and especially aspects of those

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.530
that intersect with my
interest in indigeneity

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:27.180
and newer forms, post-colonial
interpretive approaches.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.493
Two key moments for me

00:07:28.493 --> 00:07:30.960
among these were classes that I audited

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.420
on translation and enculturation,

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:37.890
and my visit to the mission archives

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:41.430
at Akrofi-Christaller
Institute of Theology.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.840
I think that they opened up for me

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.210
a whole new world of interpretation

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:49.500
that is rooted in
European-led translation,

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.240
while also exposing me to

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.800
the potential of language

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.890
and language theory, translation theory,

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.110
for the success of my entire project.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.814
Thank you very much.

00:07:59.814 --> 00:08:03.064
(audience applauding)

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:09.960
- Okay, hi everyone.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:10.793
My name is Adriana,

00:08:10.793 --> 00:08:13.020
I'm a PhD student in sociology.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:14.370
And today, I'll be sharing

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:15.870
with you all my project,

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.262
titled "Post Deportation Repeat Migration:

00:08:18.262 --> 00:08:20.017
"Intent to Remigrate Among

00:08:20.017 --> 00:08:22.110
"Recently Deported Central
American Immigrants."

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:24.570
And I presented my working paper

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.700
with the support of RITM this past summer.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.250
So despite their limited
access to healthcare

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.860
and exclusion from
mainstream institutions,

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:33.330
Latinos in the US display

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.400
lower mortality and morbidity rates

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.830
than their native and white counterparts.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:40.110
And this health advantage
is often described

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.330
as the so-called
"Hispanic health paradox,"

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.160
and it's believed to be a result

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:45.810
of the selection of immigrants

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:48.180
who migrate to and from the US.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.680
For instance, research finds that

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.840
healthier people are
more likely to migrate,

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:52.980
and those who are unhealthy are

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:54.330
more likely to return home

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:55.890
to seek medical treatment,

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:58.110
family support, or to die.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.390
However, changes in immigration policy

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:02.130
and enforcement has shaped selection

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:03.390
into return migration,

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:05.340
as immigrants can either voluntarily

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:09.030
or involuntarily return
home through deportation.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:10.290
But despite the record number

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.750
of deportations in the past two decades,

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:13.980
the health status of immigrants

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:15.210
who return to the US

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.970
after being deported
remains largely unknown.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:19.890
And this is critical to understand

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:20.880
because even though there are

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.620
significant legal repercussions

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:23.760
for attempting to return

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.533
to the US after deportation,

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:29.400
research continues to find

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.610
that deportees who are deported will be

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:33.690
more than likely to return

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:35.640
to the US sometime in the future.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.620
So my study takes a step towards capturing

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.390
the complexities of immigrant health

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:40.950
by examining the health status of

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:42.900
recently deported Central
American immigrants

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:45.450
who intend to return to the US relative

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:46.710
to their peers who will stay

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:48.030
in their country of origin.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:51.030
And I drawn data from
the survey of migration

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:52.560
in the southern border of Mexico,

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:54.150
also known as Emif Sur,

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:55.650
which is a cross-sectional survey

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:57.090
that is highly representative

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:58.650
of the Salvadoran, Honduran,

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.240
and Guatemalan adults

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.100
who are detained and deported

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.210
back to Central America

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.577
between 2012 and 2020.

00:10:05.577 --> 00:10:07.560
And overall, I find that deportees

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:09.690
who intend to return to the US are

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.880
more likely to say that
they have poor health,

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:13.860
relative to their healthier peers who plan

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.420
on staying in Central America.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.292
And I also find that deportees,

00:10:17.292 --> 00:10:19.560
who report negative experiences

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.660
during their apprehension and detention,

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:24.330
are more likely to have poor health.

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:25.650
So these findings suggest

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:27.330
that the health selection...

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.460
That health selection is a complex process

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:31.260
that cannot offer a simple explanation

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.330
for the health outcome of Latinos.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.400
In particular, this could be a result

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.100
of the Hispanic Health
Paradox being developed

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.720
at a different historical moment,

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.280
and it also may fall short

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:42.690
in explaining the health outcomes

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:43.910
of contemporary immigrants

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:46.260
in an era of mass deportation.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:48.570
And as punitive immigration
policies continue

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:50.970
to change and deportations
continue to rise,

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:52.830
it's important to explicitly examine

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:55.050
the population movement and selection

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:57.510
of what I call "post-deportation
repeat migrants,"

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:58.980
to further understand how

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:02.010
they navigate life
after their deportation.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:03.251
Thank you.

00:11:03.251 --> 00:11:06.418
(audience applauding)

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:13.073
- Hi everyone.

00:11:13.073 --> 00:11:14.640
I am Diana,

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:15.960
and I'm a second year

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:17.823
in the history PhD program.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:20.580
Gonzalo Gonzalez,

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:24.180
the subject of the right
hand article over there,

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.540
was a working class Mexican
revolutionary and baker

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:30.780
who migrated to New York
City during the 1920s.

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:32.130
Shortly after being laid off

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:34.380
from his bakery job in 1929,

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:35.850
he became an active member

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:38.160
of the Communist Party's Latina chapter

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.210
in New York.

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:40.590
In the summer of 1930,

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:42.748
he was assassinated by the NYPD

00:11:42.748 --> 00:11:44.760
at a march that protested the death

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.800
of a black comrade who
had also been killed

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:48.394
by the police.

00:11:48.394 --> 00:11:51.030
Around the time that
Gonzalo was assassinated,

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:54.210
he was one of over 4,000 Mexicans living

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:55.620
in New York City.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.870
Mexicans represented
less than five percent

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.060
of the city's Latina population,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:01.980
a relatively smaller percentage compared

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:03.270
to that of other ethnic groups

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:05.747
from Latin America and the Caribbean.

00:12:05.747 --> 00:12:07.740
However, the life and legacy

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:09.450
of Gonzalez demonstrate

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.850
that Mexicans held visible
and meaningful roles

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:14.130
in the city's labor and leftist movements

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:16.500
of the post-World War I era.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:17.760
While Gonzalez was one of

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.070
the more remarkable organizers

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.580
in the city,

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:22.920
hundreds of other working
class Mexican migrants were

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:25.560
also active members in leftist labor

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:27.990
and political organizations.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:30.750
Gonzalez's narrative
guides my current research

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:33.030
on radical Mexican political culture

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:35.100
in post-World War I New York.

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:36.900
Over the summer, I became interested

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:38.670
in taking a more trans-local approach

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:40.770
by identifying connections or networks

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:42.060
that may have been established

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:45.060
between leftist Latina or Mexican chapters

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:45.893
in New York City,

00:12:45.893 --> 00:12:50.160
and the Mexican Communist
Party, or the MCP.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.360
With support from RITM,

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.850
I spent some time in Mexico City visiting

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:57.960
El Centro de Estudios del
Movimiento Obrero y Socialista.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:12:59.790
During my archival visits,

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:02.670
I sorted through the
periodicals of El Machete,

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:05.730
which was the MCPs official newspaper

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.580
between 1924 to 1938.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:10.380
I came across a handful of articles

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.510
that confirmed an ongoing dialogue

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:13.890
between Mexican leftists

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.750
in New York and Mexico.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:16.830
In some instances,

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:19.650
Mexican New Yorkers
invited leaders of the MCP

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:22.350
to deliver speeches and conventions.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:23.850
In other occasions,

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.950
Mexican communists in New York organized

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:28.110
mutual aid events aimed

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:30.240
at fundraising for El Machete,

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.460
as shown in the bottom left.

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:34.200
Perhaps the most exciting moment

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.720
of transnational leftist encounters

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:39.360
that I came across was
when Hernán Laborde,

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:40.680
director of El Machete,

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:43.800
attended a commemoration event in 1936,

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.570
honoring the six year anniversary

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:47.160
of Gonzalez's death,

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:49.320
discussed on the right hand article.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.510
The periodicals that I
came across generated

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.610
important questions for my research.

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.507
For example, "to what extent did these

00:13:55.507 --> 00:13:57.847
"ongoing trans-local encounters shape

00:13:57.847 --> 00:13:59.107
"radical leftist movements

00:13:59.107 --> 00:14:01.477
"in New York City and Mexico City?

00:14:01.477 --> 00:14:04.420
"What were the contours of
this dialectical process?"

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:06.480
I look forward to
addressing these questions

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.910
in my ongoing social history research

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:11.910
on Gonzalez and the lives
of working class Mexicans

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:13.980
in post-World War I, New York.

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:15.527
Thank you.

00:14:15.527 --> 00:14:18.777
(audience applauding)

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:24.450
- Okay, hi everyone.

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:27.951
I'm Sophia, fourth year
in sociology and AfAm.

00:14:27.951 --> 00:14:29.407
The title of my project is

00:14:29.407 --> 00:14:31.860
"How Youth Creates Race
in the 21st Century."

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:34.020
Really, it's an attempt
to look differently

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:35.640
at the increased vulnerability

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:36.780
that young black people

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.280
and others of color face

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:40.800
in the wake of both 2020s racial violence

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:42.510
and the neglect of young people

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:44.610
in the national response to COVID-19.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.470
This comes especially as conflict arises

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:49.050
between racial and
youth-related public desire.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:50.820
So for example, the premature return

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:52.110
to unsafe school conditions,

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:54.870
justified by keeping afloat minority

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:56.160
and adolescent development,

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.500
but then condemning the
teaching of black history

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.140
with claims of being
unsuitable for children.

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:02.250
In light of these tensions,

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.890
I explore how black youth
in particular manage

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:06.300
these ideological conflicts

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:08.430
between constructions of race and youth,

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:10.800
and the impact of that relationship

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:12.870
on policy and sense of self.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:14.400
So this slide today represents two

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.230
of three parts to the project.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.360
The first is archivally tracing

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.790
what I see as this
relationship historically;

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:22.920
How do ideas about race infuse ideas

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.420
about what it means to be young?

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.490
So I trace the rise of race science

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:28.230
and its view on adolescent development,

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:30.030
how fears of peer pressure intersected

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.410
with fears of school integration,

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:32.243
things like that.

00:15:32.243 --> 00:15:35.250
And then, the second part is interviews

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:36.150
with young black people.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:38.700
So I used my RTM funding
to compensate them,

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:41.100
so I don't have pictures
of them obviously.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:42.690
But I wanted to talk about one shift

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:43.740
that I found between

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:46.350
these 20th century management tactics

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:47.520
that I found in the archives

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:50.220
and what I'm noticing
now in my interviewees,

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:54.330
because the 21st century "Trayvon
Generation," so to speak,

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:55.680
is a coming-of-age process

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.180
that's actually characterized by

00:15:57.180 --> 00:15:59.714
the ubiquity of racial violence online.

00:15:59.714 --> 00:16:02.370
So youth of color have always held

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:03.720
a voice in their communities

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:04.860
and networked with each other

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:06.870
over racial issues through media,

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.750
and how they spoke about
these racial issues was tied

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:10.890
to ideas about youth.

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:12.930
So this poem on the right was written

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:15.030
by a 15 year old after World War II

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:16.650
and racial campaigns around that.

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:18.090
She was writing in the junior section

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:20.235
of the Chicago Defender,
a black newspaper.

00:16:20.235 --> 00:16:21.960
This picture in the middle was

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:23.190
a panel of black teens

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:24.990
who was invited to speak

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:27.420
on a TV show about youth delinquency

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:29.910
and its racial associations in 1965,

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:32.162
so around different racial riots.

00:16:32.162 --> 00:16:35.100
But now, during events of racial violence,

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:36.900
the actual form of communication

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:38.130
or media is appearing

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:39.540
to have an increased impact

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:41.760
on the approach to coming-of-age

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:43.860
in comparison to the event itself, right?

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:45.060
So the social media accounts

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.460
that you make as an
adolescent rite of passage,

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:49.500
that cultivate your friendships and so on.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.120
So this quote at the bottom comes

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:52.860
from an interviewee who was talking

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:54.270
about a police brutality incident

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:56.070
that happened the same
weekend as his prom,

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:57.690
and how his Twitter feed was

00:16:57.690 --> 00:16:59.790
really in clutter because
of the different types

00:16:59.790 --> 00:17:01.110
of posts that were being shared.

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:02.857
And he says, "It's like everyone's

00:17:02.857 --> 00:17:04.597
"on Twitter sharing pictures of prom,

00:17:04.597 --> 00:17:05.977
"and I'm here on Twitter sharing pictures

00:17:05.977 --> 00:17:07.117
"of a dead person.

00:17:07.117 --> 00:17:08.617
"They're like, "But this is our youth."

00:17:08.617 --> 00:17:11.130
"Like okay, but this is mine."

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:12.720
So I'm really just interested in knowing

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:14.160
how we can pay better attention

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:16.830
to ideals about what constitutes youth,

00:17:16.830 --> 00:17:18.690
how might ideas of race be informing that,

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:20.220
and ways we might not be noticing

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:23.040
that could be causing
strife and overwhelm.

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:24.450
'Cause I really think knowing more

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:26.190
about how these relations are...

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:28.170
Relationships are produced
can kind of give us

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:29.880
another angle to dismantle them from.

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:30.713
Thank you.

00:17:32.039 --> 00:17:35.289
(audience applauding)

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:41.940
- Hi everyone.

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:45.170
I have notes but I don't
really like reading from notes,

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:47.373
so I'm gonna try to
just do it from memory.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:52.141
So Latinx folks here

00:17:52.141 --> 00:17:54.360
in the United States are the rapid...

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:56.040
Most rapid growing group

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:57.750
to convert to Islam.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:00.783
And I'm focusing on Mexico specifically.

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:05.100
Pretty much a lot of the major cities

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:08.220
in Mexico have mosques and musallas,

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:10.820
which are like small prayer rooms.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:13.920
But what I was fascinated by, to hear,

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.428
was that in Chiapas,

00:18:15.428 --> 00:18:17.857
San Cristóbal de las Casas, Chiapas,

00:18:17.857 --> 00:18:21.150
in a rural area, there's
an indigenous group,

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:24.120
Tzotzil Mayans, that were
rapidly converting to Islam,

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:26.940
and that really captured my attention.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:28.810
So there are roughly about 400

00:18:28.810 --> 00:18:30.990
or 500 Tzotzil indigenous people

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:32.120
who converted to Islam

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.170
in the year 1995.

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.420
So from 1995 to present,

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.350
there's roughly about 400 to 500 Muslims.

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:42.633
They live in a majority Catholic area.

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:45.960
Many of them were either Catholic

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:47.550
or evangelical Christians

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:49.260
before they converted to Islam.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:50.107
So my question was,

00:18:50.107 --> 00:18:52.597
"What led this specific community

00:18:52.597 --> 00:18:54.209
"to convert to Islam?

00:18:54.209 --> 00:18:56.737
"Living in a rural part of Mexico

00:18:56.737 --> 00:18:59.197
"where Catholicism is
the mainstream religion,

00:18:59.197 --> 00:19:02.130
"what attracted them to Islam?"

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:06.210
And also, the peculiar
thing about this group is

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.580
they converted across a span

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:11.550
of three generations
and within one family.

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:13.883
And so, that was
interesting to me as well.

00:19:13.883 --> 00:19:16.740
And so when I visited
Chiapas over the summer,

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:18.930
I was able to conduct roughly

00:19:18.930 --> 00:19:20.760
about 30 oral interviews,

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:22.605
and I was able to live alongside

00:19:22.605 --> 00:19:25.290
a lot of the community members there,

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:27.600
'cause there's five different
Muslim communities there

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:29.935
and so I was able to visit each one.

00:19:29.935 --> 00:19:33.600
And so, how did Islam come to Chiapas?

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.410
A Spanish Muslim by the name
of Nafia came to Spain...

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:39.960
From Spain to Mexico in 1994,

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:41.370
to Chiapas specifically.

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:44.880
And he wanted to convert Comandante Marcos

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:45.840
and the Zapatistas.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:47.880
So he's a revolutionary himself,

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:49.440
so he's like, "I wanna combine

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.877
"this revolution and Islam

00:19:51.877 --> 00:19:52.950
"and join forces."

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.200
Well, Comandante Marcos wasn't having it.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:57.420
So then, he went to other Zapatistas

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.480
and he was successful in converting them.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:02.790
And so, actually, Mohammed Amin,

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.050
who was a Zapatista,

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:06.390
he was the first to convert to Islam.

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.400
So that's another question
that I was asking,

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.310
connecting Zapatismo and
that revolutionary spirit,

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:13.740
was there like a
connection to Islam there?

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:16.350
I found that there was.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:18.150
I mean, I don't have a lot
of time to get into it,

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:20.040
but that's one of the questions

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:21.920
I wanna further explore.

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:23.820
The main reason reason I gathered

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:25.320
why people converted to Islam was

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:26.430
that they were really attracted

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:28.440
to the fact that it didn't seem

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.960
so much as like a religion to them.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:33.184
They said they didn't feel like they had

00:20:33.184 --> 00:20:34.890
to adhere to rules and strictness,

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:36.300
it was more of like a way of life

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:38.520
and it was monotheistic.

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:40.950
Here in the photo that
I have on the screen,

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:42.960
this is a photo of four women on Eid,

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:44.550
which is a Muslim religious holiday.

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:45.383
And as you can see,

00:20:45.383 --> 00:20:48.754
they're dressed in their
traditional Tzotzil clothing,

00:20:48.754 --> 00:20:52.774
and while they're wearing
their hijabs/rebozos,

00:20:52.774 --> 00:20:54.870
I was really interested in

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:56.400
the connection between the hijab

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:59.160
and if they found that it
was foreign, or what...

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:01.290
Like, how did they feel living in Mexico

00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:02.580
and observing the hijab.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:04.530
And they said that basically

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:06.720
their mothers and their
grandmothers wore rebozos,

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:07.950
some of them wore rebozos

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:08.940
when they were younger.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:10.860
And so, wearing the hijab didn't seem

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:12.750
something like too far off

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:13.830
or foreign to them.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:14.880
And it's kind of cool

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:17.490
'cause I don't think in this picture,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:22.470
but some of them wear their
traditional rebozos still

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:23.940
as their hijabs.

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:25.212
And so...

00:21:25.212 --> 00:21:26.820
Yeah, and so another...

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.950
When I interviewed a Muslim leader there

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:30.090
by the name of Ibrahim,

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:31.470
I asked him if there was a connection

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:33.090
to indigeneity and Islam,

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:34.560
and he said that his grandfather

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:36.210
actually converted to Islam

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:38.280
because he found a lot of similarities

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:39.540
with the religion of Islam

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:40.740
to his indigenous roots.

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:42.660
Just to give you one example is

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:45.090
the prostration that Muslims perform

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:46.890
during their five daily prayers.

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:48.420
He said that his grandfather was

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:51.780
already performing some of those

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:55.950
similar prostrations during
his prayers following

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:57.360
his indigenous faith.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:00.060
And so, it resonated with his grandfather,

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:01.320
and that's how him and his

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:04.224
whole entire family converted to Islam.

00:22:04.224 --> 00:22:06.060
And what I found most interesting

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:07.470
during my research trip was to see

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:09.990
how Zapatista revolutionaries
were converting to Islam,

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:13.837
but also how women found
a lot of, I'm quoting...

00:22:13.837 --> 00:22:15.747
"Liberation," "freedom."

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:20.400
And I mean, when I heard them...

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:21.570
It's their words, not mine,

00:22:21.570 --> 00:22:22.950
but when I heard them say that,

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:23.970
it was interesting to me

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:25.380
because they said the outside world

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.000
of Mexico sees them as oppressed,

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:28.777
but they're like, "We actually feel like

00:22:28.777 --> 00:22:30.930
"we're not oppressed at all."

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:34.530
And so, what I hope to do

00:22:34.530 --> 00:22:36.180
on my next trip is Omar,

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:38.010
who's a form former Zapatista,

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:43.010
he has a room full of old
newspaper clippings and articles,

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:44.550
and they're all like...

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:46.890
I mean, they're filled up to here

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:48.120
and I didn't have enough time

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:49.350
to go through all of them,

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:51.090
but I'm curious to just learn more

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:52.410
about the Zapatistas

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:54.480
and their conversions to Islam,

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:57.180
and also just about this
indigenous community

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:59.220
that is continuing to...

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:01.020
Islam is growing within

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:03.573
such a small place like
San Cristóbal de las Casas.

00:23:04.862 --> 00:23:08.112
(audience applauding)

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:16.920
- Hi everyone, my name is Cristian.

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:19.863
I'm sixth year PhD in history.

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:24.360
So yeah, this summer,

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:28.440
I spent some time
consulting some archives,

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:30.780
specifically the Standard
Food Company Archives

00:23:31.629 --> 00:23:32.783
in New Orleans, in Tulane,

00:23:33.993 --> 00:23:38.817
and the (indistinct)
Collections in Princeton.

00:23:40.292 --> 00:23:43.800
So yeah, that's just the quote that you...

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:44.670
I will translate in a few,

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:47.910
but I wanted to speak more broadly

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:49.608
about another event.

00:23:49.608 --> 00:23:51.690
In the summer of 1983,

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:54.300
James Carney, later known as Padre Lupe,

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:56.730
a US Jesuit priest turned guerilla,

00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:58.230
left a Nicaraguan border village

00:23:58.230 --> 00:24:00.900
to Olancho, Honduras,
near the Patuca River,

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:03.600
with a 96 men gorilla group called

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:06.510
the Central American
Revolutionary Workers Party, PRTC,

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:08.730
led by former Dr. Jose Maria Reyes Mata,

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:10.770
who was once a friend of Che Guevara.

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:12.840
A few weeks later, around mid-September,

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:15.840
Padre Lupe and most of
his comrades were captured

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:16.920
and summarily executed by

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:20.910
the CIA-trained Honduran
Special Forces Command, or SFC.

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:22.380
And so, whether that's...

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:23.550
The death of most of

00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:25.765
the Honduran members
didn't get much attention.

00:24:25.765 --> 00:24:27.600
US and Honduran...

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:31.050
The death of Padre Lupe, a US citizen,

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:32.940
certainly caught the attention.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:35.240
Multiple accounts of his
death still remain...

00:24:36.840 --> 00:24:37.673
Sorry.

00:24:37.673 --> 00:24:39.192
While the deaths of

00:24:39.192 --> 00:24:40.662
the Honduran members didn't get attention,

00:24:40.662 --> 00:24:42.240
the death of Lupe did,

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:45.270
and the Honduran authorities were keen

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:46.260
on suppressing the details

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:48.030
of the death of Padre Lupe

00:24:48.030 --> 00:24:49.770
because he was a US citizen.

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:52.380
Multiple accounts of his
death remained plausible.

00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:54.690
One being he was shot
with a 45 caliber pistol

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.280
along with Reyes Mata,

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.950
another one saying that
he died by malaria,

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:00.420
another one decapitated,

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:02.669
another one tortured to death,

00:25:02.669 --> 00:25:05.820
another one thrown out from a helicopter.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:06.810
However, when the bodies of

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:07.950
the other insurgents washed out

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:11.308
onto the riverbanks of the Patuca River,

00:25:11.308 --> 00:25:13.500
the authorities remained silent,

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:14.550
all while claiming that he died

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:16.470
from starvation and illness.

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:19.770
The precise fate of Padre Lupe
remains unknown to this day.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:21.300
Whether claimed by the unpredictable wrath

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:23.370
of nature and disease,

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:24.540
or by the covert designs

00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:26.280
of a CIA-trained operations,

00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:28.110
his story remains largely untold,

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:30.450
shrouded in layers of
intrigue and silence.

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:31.830
It's a silence that speaks volumes

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:33.600
about the very nature
of Honduran (indistinct)

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:35.160
of the Cold War narratives.

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:36.420
His hidden demise mirrors

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:38.460
the paradox of Honduras itself;

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:39.990
Central to US Cold War agendas

00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:42.480
yet often relegated to
a footnote of history.

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:43.800
While its terrain in politics were

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:46.320
long deemed invaluable for US interest,

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:49.500
its true role, much like Padre
Lupe's death, went unnoticed,

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:50.430
buried under the weight

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:52.380
of the larger central
American revolutions.

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:54.210
As Padre Lupe's fate remains

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:55.140
an unsolved mystery,

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:56.197
so does the question

00:25:56.197 --> 00:25:59.647
"how has a nation so
integral been overlooked

00:25:59.647 --> 00:26:01.915
"in US Cold War history?"

00:26:01.915 --> 00:26:05.550
That's the story of Honduras

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:09.930
and how it's lacked a lot
of historical production.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.840
But within that, a bigger
question that I had is

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:14.880
how you lose stories like these,

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:16.860
of Santo Centeno Garcia,

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:19.890
who was a black garifuna communist

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:21.666
and organizer in the 1950s,

00:26:21.666 --> 00:26:26.430
who was part of the larger labor movements

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:27.270
in the 1950s,

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:29.640
was also part of a anti-racist
organization called

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:32.250
the Abraham Lincoln Culture Society.

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:36.998
And so, when the product of this lack

00:26:36.998 --> 00:26:39.150
of historical production has

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:41.670
also silenced these histories

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:44.160
of working people,
working indigenous people,

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:48.480
but also working black people in Honduras.

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:52.110
So yeah, that's my intro into that.

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:54.390
My dissertation is obviously
much more complicated.

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:55.770
But yeah, that's what I wanted

00:26:55.770 --> 00:26:57.027
to leave everyone with,

00:26:57.027 --> 00:26:57.873
and thank you.

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:04.193
Take questions.
(audience applauding)

00:27:07.740 --> 00:27:09.090
- Okay, hi everyone.

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:11.070
I'm Junhan, a second year master student

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.080
who is focused on
environmental anthropology

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:14.490
at School of Environment.

00:27:14.490 --> 00:27:17.460
My research examined the
indigenous environmentalism

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:20.040
on the Tibetan plateau in China.

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:21.690
My research interest comes from

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:23.730
a contradictory observation

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:26.100
when I work with Tibetan
conservation NGOs.

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:27.273
And this is me,

00:27:27.273 --> 00:27:29.520
this is my main interlocutor.

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:32.460
And first, the homogeneous interpretation

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:34.320
of Tibetan environmentalism

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:36.420
by Chinese conservationists

00:27:36.420 --> 00:27:38.377
simply as "Tibetan people believes

00:27:38.377 --> 00:27:40.710
"in sacred mountains and lakes."

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:43.800
And then, the very diverse
practices, discourses,

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:46.290
of Tibetan conservation NGOs.

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:48.690
Each NGO's articulations about why

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.967
and how we should protect
nature is very different.

00:27:51.967 --> 00:27:55.170
This made me curious about one question,

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:57.240
how some critical things,

00:27:57.240 --> 00:28:00.390
such as indigeneity, politics, science,

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:03.960
are articulated in these
NGOs conservation projects,

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:07.230
and what we can learn about
human nature coexistence

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:09.930
from this complex landscape.

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:12.510
So this summer, I went
back to Tibetan Plateau

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:14.460
and stayed for nearly three months

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:16.110
in four NGOs.

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:19.020
And three of them were
led by Buddhist monks

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:21.789
and one was led by Tibetan herder.

00:28:21.789 --> 00:28:24.630
It was quite an enjoyable journey.

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:27.060
I had a chance to visit some old friends,

00:28:27.060 --> 00:28:28.530
and met many new faces

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:31.860
in these NGOs and
conservation conferences.

00:28:31.860 --> 00:28:34.620
I did interviews with these NGOs leaders,

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:35.550
like these monks,

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:38.490
and also did participant observation

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:40.800
in raptor monitoring, ecotourism,

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:42.663
and other conservation projects.

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:47.790
Three of my primary founding
findings can be summarized as,

00:28:47.790 --> 00:28:51.059
first, the adaptability of indigeneity

00:28:51.059 --> 00:28:53.490
in Tibetan conservation.

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:56.190
It very much relies on
environmental leaders'

00:28:56.190 --> 00:29:00.990
continuous translation between
Han Chinese and Tibetan,

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:03.210
science, and tradition, and others.

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:04.890
Second, a vital landscape

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:06.704
of Tibetan environmentalism

00:29:06.704 --> 00:29:10.770
where diverse practices
and discourse coexist

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:12.690
through similar cultural structure

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:16.410
but very different sociopolitical context,

00:29:16.410 --> 00:29:19.860
and also personal
propositions of these leaders.

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:21.310
Third, general (indistinct) ,

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:25.020
conservation knowledge
of people or nature,

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:27.390
co-produced by these NGOs.

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:28.920
It might be the next stages

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:31.920
of human nature coexistence for us,

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:35.310
while the current is more
likely people and nature.

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:37.440
In short, I hope the result of

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.090
this research can making voice

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:42.210
for indigenous people
on the Tibetan plateau,

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:45.120
and also provide alternatives

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:47.280
to the global conservation.

00:29:47.280 --> 00:29:48.813
Thank you.

00:29:48.813 --> 00:29:52.063
(audience applauding)

00:29:59.070 --> 00:29:59.903
- Hi everybody.

00:29:59.903 --> 00:30:02.820
I'm Alan, a fourth year PhD student

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:05.430
in the history of architecture program.

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:07.620
My dissertation examines
the contested logics

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:09.840
of urban development on
the Texas-Mexico border

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:11.700
in the latter half of the 20th century,

00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:14.010
and focuses on the role
of design professionals,

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:15.810
namely architects, engineers,

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:17.036
and urban planners,

00:30:17.036 --> 00:30:18.600
and the roles they played in

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:22.170
the dynamics of the region's
interstate political economy.

00:30:22.170 --> 00:30:23.910
Amid post-war reconstruction

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:26.700
and a global economic development,

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:28.050
the design professions established

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:29.460
newfound footholds in society

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:31.410
by leveraging technical knowledge

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:32.490
into innovative forms

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:34.860
of managerial expertise.

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:36.180
With its expertise in hand,

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:37.800
the design of the built environment became

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:40.050
an essential aspect of
the function of government

00:30:40.050 --> 00:30:43.710
and access to an
interconnected global economy.

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:46.183
With the assistance of RITM,

00:30:46.183 --> 00:30:47.580
I was able to conduct two weeks

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:48.870
of archival research at

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:51.990
the University of Texas' Benson
Latin American collection,

00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:55.020
Alexander Architectural Archives.

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:56.700
Specifically, the
organizations I was looking

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:59.730
at were the American Friend
Service Committee Collections,

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:04.650
the Rodman C Rockefeller Mexico-US
Business Committee files,

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:06.180
as well as the collection

00:31:06.180 --> 00:31:07.830
of the American Planning Association,

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:11.430
and a planning professor
named Hugo Leipziger,

00:31:11.430 --> 00:31:12.690
who was one of the founders

00:31:12.690 --> 00:31:15.540
of UT's planning school.

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:19.170
The archives, as we
can see from the image,

00:31:19.170 --> 00:31:21.540
presented a kaleidoscopic vision

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:24.030
of what the US-Mexico was imagined to be

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:26.917
between the years in 1960 and 1990.

00:31:26.917 --> 00:31:28.980
On one end of the spectrum,

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:31.050
we see this vision includes

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:33.240
the aspiration of modernist architects

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:36.300
and local chambers of commerce projecting

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:38.310
a certain image of modernity

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:42.412
and prosperity into
the border's landscape.

00:31:42.412 --> 00:31:43.740
And on the other,

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:47.190
we find what could be considered

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:49.760
a certain form of urban informality

00:31:49.760 --> 00:31:54.150
that presupposes a economic structure

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:58.230
that requires a certain underclass.

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:00.390
As border industrialization
attracted inhabitants

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:03.300
and capital from both
nations and elsewhere,

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:06.300
urban populations and their
economic impacts ballooned

00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:08.521
well beyond the scope devised

00:32:08.521 --> 00:32:10.290
by mid-century planners.

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:11.484
As I see it,

00:32:11.484 --> 00:32:14.340
urban informality itself is

00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:15.360
the dialectical foil

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:18.270
to modernist development on the border.

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:19.170
And if anything,

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:20.310
we have to consider

00:32:20.310 --> 00:32:23.612
these two aspects of the built environment

00:32:23.612 --> 00:32:25.323
in tension with each other.

00:32:26.203 --> 00:32:28.530
These disjunctures that we're seeing here

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:31.605
up on the screen beg the question of,

00:32:31.605 --> 00:32:34.447
"Do architects and planners
consider themselves

00:32:34.447 --> 00:32:36.577
"woefully ignorant of the effects

00:32:36.577 --> 00:32:39.014
"of their architectural impact

00:32:39.014 --> 00:32:40.177
"or can we consider this

00:32:40.177 --> 00:32:42.670
"a dereliction of professional duties

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:45.510
"and civic engagement?"

00:32:45.510 --> 00:32:46.893
But any questions?

00:32:48.027 --> 00:32:51.277
(audience applauding)

