00:34:27.938 --> 00:34:32.938
pertaining to black women, lesbians,

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:34.350
black people more broadly.

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:37.800
And so what myself and other fellows did,

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:40.230
we went down to Berlin

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:41.310
and stayed there for three weeks,

00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:42.690
and we were part of the project of trying

00:34:42.690 --> 00:34:45.270
to digitize a lot of
the different magazines

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:48.120
that came into our hands.

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:50.128
And we went to different places

00:34:50.128 --> 00:34:52.380
and different spaces to connect

00:34:52.380 --> 00:34:54.600
with black Germans.

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:58.020
So for example, we went
to a place called EOTO,

00:34:58.020 --> 00:35:00.300
which stands for "each one teach one."

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:01.500
And so, essentially,

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:03.090
it was a learning experience

00:35:03.090 --> 00:35:06.840
where we got to learn more about Germany,

00:35:06.840 --> 00:35:08.490
or black Germany particularly.

00:35:08.490 --> 00:35:10.050
And in terms of how this relates

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:12.081
to my own research,

00:35:12.081 --> 00:35:15.750
it was very fascinating to hear

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:19.140
the stories and experiences
of black Germans,

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:20.550
and how they were similar

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:24.120
and different to the
black British context.

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:29.120
And so, if one example
that one of the German...

00:35:29.670 --> 00:35:32.190
Afro-German people that
we interacted with,

00:35:32.190 --> 00:35:33.750
she would mention that in Germany,

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:36.037
people would say to her,

00:35:36.037 --> 00:35:37.803
"Oh, your German is so good.

00:35:37.803 --> 00:35:40.200
"How can you speak such a good German?"

00:35:40.200 --> 00:35:41.300
And she'll say, "well, I'm from here.

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:42.360
"I was born here."

00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:45.363
And the refrain will always be,

00:35:45.363 --> 00:35:47.430
"But where you really from?"

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:50.010
And so, that was something
that was definitely

00:35:50.010 --> 00:35:51.420
something I connect to,

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:52.770
'cause in the UK,

00:35:52.770 --> 00:35:54.360
there has been a black population there

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:55.860
for a very long time,

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:59.070
but often our connection to

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:01.380
the nation is denied and repudiated.

00:36:01.380 --> 00:36:05.550
so there's a lot of
different connections there

00:36:05.550 --> 00:36:06.900
that I can relate to.

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:08.940
But the project more broadly is

00:36:08.940 --> 00:36:10.590
something that is gonna continue.

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:12.750
And on Friday and Saturday,

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:15.000
as you can see from the poster

00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:15.900
that's going around,

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:16.860
and you can turn it around

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:19.050
as well to stuff at the back
to show you who's coming.

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:22.470
But yeah, on Friday, you
are all welcome to come,

00:36:22.470 --> 00:36:26.160
and we will be having different
discussions and talks,

00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:28.140
and you're gonna have different people,

00:36:28.140 --> 00:36:29.610
different black scholars

00:36:29.610 --> 00:36:31.320
and activists from Europe,

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:33.750
coming to speak about their work

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:35.808
and black European studies.

00:36:35.808 --> 00:36:38.160
And for example, Gloria Wekker,

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:39.150
who's quite well known,

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:41.760
she's a black Dutch
feminist anthropologist

00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:42.600
who'll be there.

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:44.760
And there'll be many other people as well.

00:36:44.760 --> 00:36:46.380
And I'll be there as well.

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:49.440
But that was a quick little snapshot

00:36:49.440 --> 00:36:50.607
of what I was doing

00:36:50.607 --> 00:36:54.003
in the summer in Berlin, so yeah.

00:36:55.014 --> 00:36:58.264
(audience applauding)

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:06.840
- Hi everyone, my name is Zoe Lee-Park.

00:37:06.840 --> 00:37:08.490
I study the Normative Theory

00:37:08.490 --> 00:37:09.660
of Environmental Justice.

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:11.850
What should environmental
justice look like?

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:13.170
The environmental justice movement,

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:14.700
if you don't know what it is,

00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:17.100
it's the poor people of color movement

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:19.920
for clean and safe environments for all.

00:37:19.920 --> 00:37:22.890
Centuries and decades of
white supremacy have resulted

00:37:22.890 --> 00:37:24.600
in many of these communities living

00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:26.850
in places that are very
toxic, very polluted.

00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:28.590
And in my review of the literature,

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:30.540
I found that there's
still a real disconnect

00:37:30.540 --> 00:37:32.880
between scholarly normative arguments

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:34.170
and then the grassroots arguments

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:36.720
that I was hearing in
my own work off campus.

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:38.160
And so, through my thesis,

00:37:38.160 --> 00:37:40.200
I sought to bridge this kind of gap.

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:43.230
So I pursued a 14 week
embedded ethnography with two

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:46.320
different environmental justice
organizations in California.

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:47.640
I am from California,

00:37:47.640 --> 00:37:49.800
these are communities I really love.

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:52.290
So during those 14
weeks I was volunteering

00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:54.420
and really learning
through that volunteering.

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:56.610
And so, for the first
seven weeks of summer,

00:37:56.610 --> 00:37:57.990
I was at Green Action for Health

00:37:57.990 --> 00:37:59.190
and Environmental Justice.

00:37:59.190 --> 00:38:00.450
They're based in San Francisco.

00:38:00.450 --> 00:38:02.700
And then, for the second
seven weeks of summer,

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:04.920
I was at the Central Coast Alliance United

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:06.150
for a Sustainable Economy,

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:07.170
or CAUSE for short.

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:08.550
And CAUSE asked for my support

00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:09.840
at the Santa Maria office.

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:12.240
Santa Maria is a predominantly
farm working community.

00:38:12.240 --> 00:38:14.130
And so, through my volunteering,

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:15.870
I learned from my communities

00:38:15.870 --> 00:38:17.250
what environmental justice

00:38:17.250 --> 00:38:18.930
and responsibilities should include.

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:20.460
And I adopted approaches

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:21.840
from grounded normative theory.

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:23.700
Grounded normative theory is outlined

00:38:23.700 --> 00:38:25.080
by Professor Brooke Ackerly.

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:27.090
And so, I sought to use a variety

00:38:27.090 --> 00:38:28.950
of different empirical methods,

00:38:28.950 --> 00:38:30.720
developing ideas recursively,

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:32.370
being inclusive of different ways

00:38:32.370 --> 00:38:33.750
of knowing in my communities,

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:35.520
and then re-articulating these findings

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:37.181
in ways back to my communities

00:38:37.181 --> 00:38:38.014
that were helpful to them.

00:38:38.014 --> 00:38:39.480
And so, I organized focus groups,

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:40.560
which were smaller discussions

00:38:40.560 --> 00:38:41.550
with my community members,

00:38:41.550 --> 00:38:44.184
and organized presentation
feedback sessions

00:38:44.184 --> 00:38:46.860
where I asked my communities for feedback

00:38:46.860 --> 00:38:47.910
on what I was finding.

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:49.500
I also attended public meetings

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:51.368
and I conducted several interviews.

00:38:51.368 --> 00:38:54.015
And I hired three research assistants,

00:38:54.015 --> 00:38:56.520
three really brilliant young women

00:38:56.520 --> 00:38:57.450
from my communities

00:38:57.450 --> 00:39:00.360
who supported the project tremendously.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:02.160
And so, the goal for the thesis is

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:04.980
to develop normative theories rooted

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:07.260
in the arguments of my community members

00:39:07.260 --> 00:39:09.180
to really help them shape policy

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:11.728
and society to towards their visions.

00:39:11.728 --> 00:39:14.670
And so now, this semester back at Yale,

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:16.380
I am learning how to analyze

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:17.880
my ethnographic field notes.

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:19.920
And as I'm analyzing my field notes,

00:39:19.920 --> 00:39:21.360
I'm combining those findings

00:39:21.360 --> 00:39:22.740
with my two other methods,

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:25.260
which is content analysis
on pieces published

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:26.340
by these organizations,

00:39:26.340 --> 00:39:28.230
and then also historical analysis

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:29.940
on the histories of my communities;

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:31.920
How those different histories have shaped

00:39:31.920 --> 00:39:34.200
the normative of arguments they're making.

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:36.210
And in the spring of 2024,

00:39:36.210 --> 00:39:38.425
I'm gonna be returning to California

00:39:38.425 --> 00:39:40.770
to share the findings of this study

00:39:40.770 --> 00:39:43.200
wherever my community
members want me to share.

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:46.113
Local council meetings
have come up as a big one.

00:39:46.950 --> 00:39:50.370
And I'm really grateful to the center

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:51.780
for all of the ongoing support,

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:53.880
especially it enabled me to hire

00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:55.380
a third research assistant.

00:39:55.380 --> 00:39:57.030
Really brilliant...

00:39:57.030 --> 00:39:58.290
Really brilliant young person.

00:39:58.290 --> 00:40:00.663
And so yeah, thankful for all the support.

00:40:01.874 --> 00:40:05.124
(audience applauding)

